From 04c0e8023df8c37bcbd275c88acaf833e0d855f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nan Xiao <nanxstats@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 21:07:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Represent absent bounds by -Inf/Inf instead of the finite
 sentinel 20

The C boundary search used a single constant EXTREMEZ = 20 as (1) the value
returned for an analysis with zero incremental spending, (2) the clamp on
Newton-Raphson iterates and (3) the iteration cap; the R layer mirrored the
sentinel with rep(-20, k), bound < 20 checks and a hard-coded 20 for skipped
analyses. This change makes an absent bound -Inf (lower, harm) or +Inf
(upper) end to end:

* gsbound.c / gsbound1.c: a side with a non-positive target is returned as
  -Inf/+Inf and its Newton iteration is skipped; the iteration cap is the
  named constant GS_MAXITER (20) and the iterate clamp GS_ZCLAMP (20) is
  kept only as a safeguard for unattainable targets (crossing boundaries),
  where R code repairs the design afterwards. The update guards an exact
  hit so that a vanishing density can no longer produce a 0/0 step and NaN
  bounds (previously possible when two analyses are very close in
  information and a target is zero).
* .C() calls pass NAOK = TRUE so that infinite bounds can be exchanged with
  C; the wrappers validate against missing values explicitly. gsDensity()
  no longer reads a NULL lower bound for one-sided designs.
* gsDesign.R, gsCP.R, gsqplot.R, sequentialPValue.R, gsSurvPower.R,
  toInteger.R: -20/20 sentinels replaced by -Inf/Inf; root-search intervals
  stay finite; iterative convergence checks use gsBoundChange(), which
  treats an unchanged infinite bound as no change (Inf - Inf is NaN).
* gsBoundCP() and the predictive power rows of gsBoundSummary() return NA
  at an analysis without a bound instead of evaluating at z = 20.
* Tests and fixtures updated; snapshot shows Inf in gsBound()/gsBound1()
  output. Numerically the change only affects the sentinel values and the
  probabilities of crossing them (below 1e-30), see NEWS.

